<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lý Thường Kiệt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1019">1019</a>–1105</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lý Thường Kiệt was a general and court official of the Lý dynasty of Đại Việt (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>). In 1075–1076 he led a pre-emptive invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, sacking Yongzhou, and in 1077 he defeated the Song counter-invasion at the Như Nguyệt river north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>. The poem 'Nam quốc sơn hà', traditionally attributed to him, is often called Vietnam's first declaration of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ly-thuong-kiet.jpg" alt="Lý Thường Kiệt" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phương Huy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C6%B0%E1%BB%A3ng_L%C3%BD_Th%C6%B0%E1%BB%9Dng_Ki%E1%BB%87t_t%E1%BA%A1i_B%E1%BA%A3o_t%C3%A0ng_l%E1%BB%8Bch_s%E1%BB%AD_qu%C3%A2n_s%E1%BB%B1_Vi%E1%BB%87t_Nam,_H%C3%A0_N%E1%BB%99i_n%C4%83m_2017_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
